      "question_text_en": "If f(x) = (3x^2 + 1)^4, what is the first derivative f'(x)?",
      "diagram_asset": "assets/images/diagrams/math_sample_diagram.svg",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "verification_status": "published",
      "source_name": "FidelLearn original demonstration content",
      "source_page": 58,
      "content_version": 1,
      "choices": [
        { "id": "c_math_007_a", "label": "A", "text_en": "12x(3x^2 + 1)^3", "is_correct": false },
        { "id": "c_math_007_b", "label": "B", "text_en": "24x(3x^2 + 1)^3", "is_correct": true },
        { "id": "c_math_007_c", "label": "C", "text_en": "4(3x^2 + 1)^3", "is_correct": false },
        { "id": "c_math_007_d", "label": "D", "text_en": "6x(3x^2 + 1)^3", "is_correct": false }
      ],
      "explanation": {
        "solution_text_en": "By Chain Rule: d/dx [u^4] = 4u^3 * u'. Here u = 3x^2 + 1 and u' = 6x. Thus f'(x) = 4(3x^2 + 1)^3 * (6x) = 24x(3x^2 + 1)^3.",
        "simpler_explanation_en": "Differentiate outer power (4) and multiply by inner derivative (6x): 4 * 6x = 24x.",
        "key_concept": "Chain rule: d/dx[g(x)^n] = n*g(x)^(n-1)*g'(x)",
        "common_pitfall": "Forgetting to multiply by the inner function derivative 6x."
      }
    },
